narrative:
Yeva's letter lists grain, wages, roofs, and the names of workers who will need winter advances. Only at the bottom, after the arithmetic, does she write one line without a number beside it.

@Yeva: I do not know whether I did right by them. I know what it cost. There is a difference, and I am tired of pretending there is not.

The ink has smudged once, then been blotted carefully dry. Her vulnerability arrives disguised as bookkeeping, because that is the language she trusts.

Petra's note contains no signature, but the last line gives her away.

@Petra: I have spent so long making certain no one could leave me first that I may have become very good at leaving. Do not mistake this for an apology. I have standards.

Outside, the broken bell finally rings once. It is not a beautiful sound. It is a sound made by someone refusing to let silence win.

Tamsin says she has heard nothing. Then she pours you a drink and tells you everything.

@Tamsin: You did not hear this from me, which is fortunate, because I only heard it from a man who swore he was dead when it happened. Northern officers are buying funeral cloth. That means they expect a war, or they expect to make one.

For once, she does not smile after delivering the punch line.
